A||||19||pontiacs4me||why do OE exhaust manifold bolts have to be such pieces of crap?||||1||
Z||000000||pontiacs4me||05-22-2001||12:53 AM||||I hate OE bolts.  I fixed my exhaust leak today.  Luckily I didnt have to remove the entire cradle, just drop it down in the front to get to the hard manifold.  Unluckly, I had 2 bolts want to get some revenge on me for some reason.  After I installed the new gasket, and began tightening the bolts, the two bolts that are farthest to the driver side decided to strip out.  Not wanting to drill and tap to a bigger size, I just caught a ride with my mom to the hardware store and bought some 5/16 18 grade 8 bolts, that were about 3/16 of an inch longer than stock.  I just forced them into there real good, fired up the car, let it cool and tightened them again real good.  <BR>Finally some peace and quiet.||||reg||1
Z||000001||wkayl||05-22-2001||08:19 AM||||A couple of years ago I replaced a manifold on my 61 Studebaker Lark V8.  I was worried until I took out the 1st bolt and found out they are stainless steel.  I <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||stsmithgt||05-22-2001||11:21 AM||||Did you leave or install that recall piece of sheet metal on the front manifold?  Just curious since mine is leaking.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||05-22-2001||04:20 PM||||tip: never ever reuse exhaust bolts. and replace them with the saturn kit, or stainless. and anti-sieze compound. Use studs and flange nuts. that way if one does stick the other will still come off. I have no idea what gm was thinking using such lite grade bolts or studs.||||reg||1
Z||000004||sjp777||05-22-2001||09:24 PM||||Be very glad they didn't break inside when you tried to take them out. They love to do that and the fix is the biggest pita I have ever encountered working on Fieros<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_2.jpg">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||cadaver||05-22-2001||09:53 PM||||I broke a forward exhaust bolt once. I just got out my drill and drilled and tapped it. It wasent too bad.||||reg||1
Z||000006||PBJ||05-22-2001||09:59 PM||||stsmithgt, be sure to re-install the heat shield. It keeps the heat of the manifolds away from the engine block-Which in a fiero is already hot enough.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||05-23-2001||07:45 AM||||I've heard the "Saturn Kit" mentioned before.  Any details?<P>Thanks,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||05-23-2001||09:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Carrolles:<BR><B>I've heard the "Saturn Kit" mentioned before.  Any details?<P>Thanks,<P>Carroll</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>s<P>Saturn dealers have a kit that uses probably grade 8 bolts with flange nuts. I havent used it but lots here have. I used stainless on mine. either way, use a dab of anti seize installing them.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||stsmithgt||05-23-2001||09:56 AM||||Check!  I just don't get how to seal it up well enough since there's only one gasket used on the manifold side and not on the other side of the heat shield.  That's why I'm getting my manifolds machined up for the best fit I can get before repairing the leak.  There has to be something to help prevent leaks between the head and the heat sheild.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||05-23-2001||09:59 AM||||I dont know what the saturn kit is - saturns also have 100% stainless steel exhaust - so maybe they use stainless head bolts?<P>  I found out the hard way from working on old VWs - the best thing to do to hold on the exhaust to the head is to use STUDS in the head - stainless is better - AND use SOFT nuts and washers.<P><BR>   then when you want to remove the exhaust manifold, if the nut looks rusty at all take a chisel to it, hit it slight off center on the side that will try to unscrew it.<P>  If its not rusted on too hard it will unscrew (this also works by the way if you have to take off - or put on - a nut you cant get a wrench on!)<P>  if it doesnt let go and unscrew then keep at it with the chisel - two or three good wacks and the nut either unscrews or it splits.<P>   Once I started doing this religiously - (not matter how clean the threads or nut looks) I never broke a stud off in an engine again.<P>   Not only does the chisel split the nut if its hopelessly siezed - if its not siezed it sends an incredible shock wave through the metal that is your best hope of breaking the rust free - so either way its the ONLY way to go.<P>   But if your exhaust is held on now with bolts - there is nothing you can do but soak it with WD40, put a box wrench on it and wack teh wrench with a hammer. Same kinda shock wave idea - not nearly as effective.<P>  wait, now that i think of it, taking something like the extender for your 3/8" sockets and putting it on the bolt and giving it a few good smacks might also help to break the rust lock free.<P>  but even if you do get them out - replace them all with new studs and nuts.||||reg||1
Z||000011||skitime||05-23-2001||10:15 AM||||stsmithgt, I just eliminated the heat sheild. They get corroded around the flanges and cause leaks.  Otherwise you need new sheilds.  One less area to leak.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||stsmithgt||05-23-2001||04:12 PM||||It seems like a flip of the coin on opinions to leave the shield or to chuck it.  I wouldn't worry about the heat getting to the block.  I'm not too worried about oil dripping down to the cat either, which is one of its purposes I think.  If anything goes wrong with my car, I'm usually all over it, except this exhaust leak needed more investigating.  I'm stopping by the machine shop today to see how my manifolds are coming.  They said they would call, but you all know how that goes.  <BR>If I can't convince myself that my exhaust will stay air tight with that shield in there, I'll chuck it and take other measures to keep the car fire free. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firetruck.gif"> <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ken Wittlief||05-23-2001||04:21 PM||||it doesnt seem right that the heat shield would go between the manifold and the head?! are you sure about this? if so you definately would need double gaskets.<P>  maybe it goes on outside the manifold? gasket on head - then manifold - then heat shield - then washers and nuts?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Carrolles||05-23-2001||04:44 PM||||The heat shield goes between the head and the exhaust manifold without a gasket.  I used the red sealer on mine and have had no leaks in 18 months so far.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Steven||05-23-2001||05:28 PM||||Ken, If you have never seen this set up before, it is talked about in here <A HREF="http://www.michiganfieroclub.org/v6recall.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.michiganfieroclub.org/v6recall.htm</A>  it show how it was installed as part of the recall.<P>------------------<BR> 85 Fiero GT<BR> 3.2 Liter Under Construction<p>[This message has been edited by Steven (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Iron Duke Wellingsley||05-24-2001||03:01 AM||||I tried the double gasket bit on my Iron Duke (head, gasket, sheild, gasket, manifold).  It required modifying one of the gaskets, was a pain to get lined up, and it leaked anyway.<P>My heat shield was missing the attachment to the block (at bottom) so the shield rattled noticeably and I had to "adjust" the heat shield near the heat stove to get rid of another rattle.<P>I want to ditch the F-ing shield. I hate the idea of it rattling and interfering in the seal between head and manifold.  On the other hand, it was installed for safety.  <P>So, it will stay on there and in time I will forget that I hate it if I don't look at it too much.||||reg||10
Z||000017||stsmithgt||05-24-2001||11:09 AM||||Yeah, you'll forget you hate it until it starts leaking again.  Then those hatefull feeling come back to haunt you.  Been there done that, am there doing that. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   That's why I'm looking for a better solution through these threads of advice and suggestions.||||reg||1
Z||000018||rogergarrison||05-24-2001||11:41 AM||||most new cars dont even have any gasket between head and exhaust manifold. Just 2 machined surfaces.||||reg||1
Z||000019||stsmithgt||05-24-2001||01:34 PM||||That's true.  I've pulled several engines apart that didn't have gaskets.  From the factory, they're  nice and clean surfaces.  Or freshly machined heads and manifolds could seal up without gaskets.  My engine is old, the two surfaces need something to compensate for the wear and tear from heat and corrosion, like a gasket.  I don't want  to pull heads to machine them just to stop a leak on a motor with 127,000 miles on it.  I want to milk this engine for all it's worth without sinking a lot more money into it.  If I didn't have an A/C, it wouldn't be such a big deal, but I do and it works great.  ||||reg||1
